`SetTimestamp` is a single forwarding call to `SetFieldUINT32Value`, so there is nothing real to speed up inside it. Any cost is in the field lookup inside the base class.

The only change is an explicit cast of the value to `FIT_UINT32`. `FIT_DATE_TIME` is a typedef for `FIT_UINT32`, so the cast changes nothing: behavior and the generated code stay exactly the same. Do not expect a measurable speedup from it.

```cpp
    void SetTimestamp(FIT_DATE_TIME timestamp)
    {
        SetFieldUINT32Value(253, static_cast<FIT_UINT32>(timestamp), 0, FIT_SUBFIELD_INDEX_MAIN_FIELD);
    }
```